Concord: Building a Real HRIS Service
Adviser notes, 2026-10-20
Decision
Build an actual HRIS service now, instead of filling the IAM catalog with imaginary apps through a demo seeder. A real service forces the cross-service contracts to be real, and it gives the frontend real data for the dashboard and profile.
Seeding shrinks, it doesn't disappear
• IAM still needs the hris app, with its Groups and Blocks, registered in the catalog.
• HRIS itself needs a few seeded employees and departments to test with.
• This is a smaller, cleaner seed, because it matches what the real service will hold.
What HRIS depends on in IAM
The gaps found in the current code show up the moment HRIS exists:
1. Block checks. HRIS needs a way to ask IAM whether a user holds a given Block. Nothing exposes this today; the edge passes only CONCORD_USER_ID.
2. Assigning offices. When HR assigns a user to a department, IAM needs an endpoint to create the membership and set the primary office. HR never writes to IAM's database.
3. Account creation. The hire flow is that HR asks IAM to create the account. That endpoint does not exist yet.
4. The /me contract. Groups and Blocks returned as objects (not flat key strings), a launch_path per app, and an is_primary marker on memberships. The frontend must not parse block keys or infer the primary office from array order.
HRIS v0 scope: keep it thin
Build only what the frontend needs to render real data:
• A database with departments, employees (with iam_user_id), and department assignments with history.
• GET /api/hr/employees/me, which also gives the profile page real data.
• An employee list gated by one Block.
• An "assign to department" action gated by another Block, which calls IAM.
Leave for later: the correction-request workflow, hiring, and reports.
Home office
The primary (home) office is decided by HRIS: HR assigns a user to an office/department, and that becomes the user's home office and dashboard. IAM cannot read HRIS's database, so IAM stores the result (for example an is_primary flag on the membership), set through an IAM endpoint that HRIS calls.
Build order
1. Settle the cross-service contracts (items 1 to 3 above).
2. Build the IAM side: the endpoints, is_primary, the new /me shape, and the hris catalog entries.
3. Add the HRIS service to compose and nginx with auth_request, using the existing /api/hr/ template.
4. Build the thin HRIS slice.
5. Point the frontend at the new /me and drop the hardcoded dashboard.
Open decision
Who owns the list of offices and departments?
• Option A: HRIS owns departments, and IAM offices mirror them by code.
• Option B: IAM keeps creating offices, and HRIS only references them.
This decides what HR's assign action calls and what the HRIS database must hold. It should be recorded in the decisions document once chosen.
Reminders
• Blocks in the frontend only show or hide UI. HRIS must enforce every Block server-side.
• OPEN-AUTH-001 (active office context) and OPEN-AUTH-003 (View dependencies) remain unresolved. The frontend renders Blocks exactly as granted and adds no View-implication logic.